/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A laser diode is a p-n junction with a very thin active layer — tens of nanometres to about 0.1 µm — between two wider-bandgap cladding layers.
   - Two things change the picture.
   - Thirty per cent per facet is enough.
   - Written as a current density, the threshold condition in the linear-gain approximation is:
   - Jth = (αi + αm) / (Γ β)
   - Jth is threshold current density in A/m² (device data sheets quote A/cm²); αi is internal loss per metre; αm = (1/2L)·ln(1/(R₁R₂)) is mirror loss per metre for cavity length L in metres and facet reflectivities R₁, R₂; Γ is the confinement 
   - A double heterostructure confines both carriers and the optical field to the thin active layer.
   - A shorter cavity raises αm and so raises Jth, but the active area shrinks, so the threshold current Ith = Jth × area falls.
   - Above threshold the light-current curve is a straight line:
   - P is emitted optical power in W; I is forward current in A; Ith is threshold current in A; ηs is slope efficiency in W/A, and ηs = ηd (hν/q) with ηd the differential quantum efficiency (a pure number, at most 1), hν the photon energy in J a
*/
