/* book exam traps: Using the whole current instead of (I − Ith): at 60 mA with ηs = 0.30 W/A you get 18 mW, not 12 mW. | Slope efficiency is a slope in W/A, not a percentage. The percentage is the differential quantum efficiency ηd, and the two differ by the factor hν/q. | Threshold current (mA) and threshold current density (A/cm²) are different quantities; Jth multiplied by active area gives Ith. | Longitudinal mode spacing comes from cavity length, transverse modes come from waveguide width. Examiners swap the two words. | Mirror loss αm and internal loss αi are separate terms that add inside Jth; a small R lowers reflectivity and raises threshold, it does not raise output. */
